list linux commands for learning devops

Absolutely. If you're learning **DevOps**, don't try to memorize every Linux command. Focus on commands you'll actually use for **servers, files, processes, networking, permissions, logs, automation, and troubleshooting**.

### 1. Basic navigation & files

```bash
pwd
ls
ls -la
cd
mkdir
rmdir
touch
cp
mv
rm
find
locate
file
stat
```

### 2. Reading & editing files

```bash
cat
less
more
head
tail
tail -f
nano
vim
```

**Important for DevOps:**

```bash
tail -f /var/log/syslog
```

Useful for watching logs in real time.

### 3. Searching & text processing

```bash
grep
grep -r
awk
sed
cut
sort
uniq
wc
tr
xargs
```

Learn these especially well:

```bash
grep
awk
sed
```

For example:

```bash
grep "ERROR" app.log
```

### 4. Permissions & users

```bash
whoami
id
who
w
su
sudo
useradd
usermod
userdel
passwd
groups
chmod
chown
chgrp
umask
```

You should understand:

```bash
chmod 755 script.sh
chown user:group file.txt
```

### 5. Processes & system resources

```bash
ps
top
htop
pgrep
pkill
kill
killall
jobs
bg
fg
nohup
free
df
du
uptime
uname
```

Very important:

```bash
ps aux
top
df -h
du -sh *
free -h
```

### 6. Services & logs

Modern Linux servers commonly use `systemd`:

```bash
systemctl
journalctl
service
```

Examples:

```bash
systemctl status nginx
systemctl restart nginx
systemctl enable nginx
journalctl -u nginx
journalctl -f
```

### 7. Networking

```bash
ip
ss
ping
curl
wget
ssh
scp
rsync
dig
nslookup
traceroute
hostname
```

Master these:

```bash
ip addr
ip route
ss -tulpn
curl
ssh
scp
rsync
```

### 8. Disk & storage

```bash
lsblk
blkid
mount
umount
df
du
fdisk
parted
```

For day-to-day DevOps:

```bash
df -h
du -sh /var/*
lsblk
